WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:09.840
This work is able to simulate an ecosystem with&nbsp;
up to 500,000 plants for 500 years. Buckle up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.160
Fellow Scholars, because we are going to see&nbsp;
simulations with tipping points, deforestation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:24.440
Yosemite valley, and the story of shrubs and&nbsp;
pines. Yes we are in for a ride with this paper.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.320
This simulation goes down to the&nbsp;
level of individual branches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.040
Awesome. And this can simulate cloud&nbsp;
formations too. And it gets better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.840
it also simulates how the&nbsp;
ecosystem responds to it.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.400
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.080
Now, we know that a good simulation paper&nbsp;
has to be able to give us interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.200
insights about the world around us.&nbsp;
Does this do that? Well, look. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.920
we see clouds everywhere above the&nbsp;
rainforest. Now, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:08.640
here comes a little deforestation, this is just&nbsp;
a simulation, no real trees were harmed at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:18.360
and look, the clouds disappeared! And, when the&nbsp;
forest slowly grows back, see? The clouds come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:27.320
back too. Why is that? Because of transpiration.&nbsp;
This means that rainforests release water vapor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.840
into the atmosphere. The trees sweat, if&nbsp;
you will. Now, this vapor rises, cools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:41.200
and condenses into clouds. As we have deforested&nbsp;
this region, we have disrupted this beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.360
dance, and thus, no more clouds. And this paper&nbsp;
can simulate all of these phenomena. Love it.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.480
Now, let’s have a more drastic experiment.&nbsp;
Here you see how this virtual ecosystem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.440
reacts to decreasing precipitation.&nbsp;
In other words, what would happen if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.680
there would be less and less rain. Things&nbsp;
are going as we expect them to go - new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.920
different kinds of species arise that&nbsp;
can thrive in this kind of climate…and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:19.480
wait what? Uh oh… what is happening? Well, Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars, this is the effect of the tipping point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:26.520
this means that the effect of some changes is&nbsp;
predictable up until a point, and after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:33.360
unpredictable, and often catastrophic changes&nbsp;
happen. And these are often irreversible.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.440
Now a slow drought commences, and look&nbsp;
at the patterns in which this shrub land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:46.240
slowly recedes. Why slowly? Because&nbsp;
they are really good at adapting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.080
the new conditions…until a&nbsp;
point, of course. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:57.840
when we do the same thing faster, rain is gone&nbsp;
quicker, the patterns are suddenly clumpier.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.000
And if we do the slow drought simulation with&nbsp;
pine trees, you remember from a previous episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.600
how they grow their roots, now these are not so&nbsp;
good at adapting to the new conditions, so even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:19.224
in the case of the slow drought, let’s see… yes,&nbsp;
they disappear in clumps. Oof. Now I know what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.224 --> 00:03:27.880
are looking for, how can the pine trees deal with&nbsp;
a fast drought. Unfortunately, they do even worse.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:34.640
And now, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because now we are going to do both and see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:42.800
happens. Add shrubs and pine trees at the same&nbsp;
time. This is a simulation after all. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:50.560
do whatever we want here. And…look at that!&nbsp;
The two species cooperate and compete at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:56.840
same time, forming these beautiful striped&nbsp;
patterns that would not appear otherwise.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:04.560
So, in such a simulation paper, we always ask,&nbsp;
this sure looks nice, but is this all for show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:11.720
or is this what would really happen in reality?&nbsp;
Well, look at this. Here are the patterns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:19.560
emerge in theoretical results in these cases,&nbsp;
and here is the simulation. Wow. Feast your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:25.880
eyes upon this incredible agreement. And&nbsp;
it gets even better. When leaving theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:33.680
behind, and comparing to real photos in&nbsp;
real-world places, wow. I am out of words.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:40.680
And we have two more experiments. First&nbsp;
is simulating how microclimates emerge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:47.440
This is a multi-species simulation where we get&nbsp;
species taking place right at the center of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:54.920
formation. These folks love a wet climate.&nbsp;
But not everyone does. These species like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:01.800
dryer weather. Let’s remove both of them.&nbsp;
This corridor then regrows these species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.120
showcasing the famous forest edge effect&nbsp;
where different species carve out their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.440
niches, responding to the subtle&nbsp;
changes in light and moisture.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:18.920
And now, if you allow me to, I could not&nbsp;
resist this one. In the shadow of Yosemite's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:24.520
majestic Half Dome, a thick forest of&nbsp;
pines whispers of nature's intricate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:30.520
dance. As we toy with the vapor in the air,&nbsp;
mimicking the breath of a changing climate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:35.840
the trees form curious ribbon-like&nbsp;
patterns across the terrain. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:42.720
suspense hangs in the air; this lush tableau&nbsp;
is on borrowed time as the forest withers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:49.840
leaving behind a haunting, arid landscape with&nbsp;
